################################################################

Restricted Model <-- In this case, 1 year of going to school increases salary by half a dollar. i.e. 2 years of experience is equivalent to 
                     1 year of school, which is equivalent to 1/2 a dollar of salary incremental. 

nlsw88$newvar <- nlsw88$yrs_school + 2*nlsw88$ttl_exp   --> create new variable that collapse 2 linear variables together. 

restricted <- lm(nlsw88$lwage ~ nlsw88$newvar)

summary(restricted) 
# Call:
# lm(formula = nlsw88$lwage ~ nlsw88$newvar)

# Residuals:
#  Min       1Q   Median       3Q      Max 
#-1.79637 -0.32172 -0.02268  0.27505  2.39896 

#Coefficients:
#               Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    
#(Intercept)   0.865430   0.042395   20.41   <2e-16 ***
#nlsw88$newvar 0.026292   0.001075   24.47   <2e-16 ***
#  ---
#  Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1

#Residual standard error: 0.5106 on 2244 degrees of freedom
#Multiple R-squared:  0.2106,	Adjusted R-squared:  0.2102 
#F-statistic: 598.6 on 1 and 2244 DF,  p-value: < 2.2e-16

anova_rest <- anova(restricted)
anova_rest
#Analysis of Variance Table

#Response: nlsw88$lwage
#                Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(>F)    
#nlsw88$newvar    1 156.08 156.080  598.62 < 2.2e-16 ***
#  Residuals     2244 585.09   0.261                      
#---
#  Signif. codes:  0 ‘***’ 0.001 ‘**’ 0.01 ‘*’ 0.05 ‘.’ 0.1 ‘ ’ 1

#Test
statistic_test <- (anova_rest$`Sum Sq`[2]-anova_unrest$`Sum Sq`[3]/1)/
                      (anova_unrest$`Sum Sq`[3]/anova_unrest$Df[3])
#1: number of restrictions and 2243: Df of Residuals in anova_unrest
#select columns rows with [row no.]
statistic_test #172.9599
                
               
#########################################################################################

Do you reject or not reject this null hypothesis at a confidence level of 95%?

pvalue <- df(statistic_test, 1, anova_unrest$Df[3])
pvalue #1.930469e-38

The p-value associated with this test is less than 0.05 (95% CI). 
Then, we can reject the null hypothesis at this confidence level. 


You can also use the car package in R and the following code to perform the test directly:
install.packages("car")
library(car)

matrixR <- c(0, -2, 1) ------> matrix notation for your hypothesis i.e beta_o = 0, beta_1+2*beta_1 = -2, beta_3,beta_4 = 1

linearHypothesis(multi, matrixR)

